## The shop

Halden HVAC & Refrigeration has been running out of a small yard on the north side of Springfield since 1987. Marcus Halden took it over from his father in 2011. Twelve people on payroll: Marcus, an office manager, a dispatcher, four senior techs, three juniors, two apprentices, one estimator who also runs commercial installs. About sixty percent residential, forty percent light commercial (small restaurants, one grocery chain, a couple of manufacturers).

They had already tried the software route. ServiceTitan came in 2019, went back out in 2021 because the interface was faster than the shop could absorb and nobody had time to babysit the migration. Since then the schedule was a whiteboard the dispatcher rewrote every morning, customer history was half in Excel and half in Marcus's head, and quotes got hand-written in the truck and typed up at night. Some quotes took three or four days to send.

<Callout title="Illustrative case">

Halden HVAC & Refrigeration is a composite shop, not a real client. Every metric, quote, and workflow beat below is written from patterns Muscar has seen in the field, sized to a plausible twelve-person operation, and labeled clearly. When we ship a real case, this label comes off and the numbers get replaced with the actual ones.

</Callout>

## Where the hours were going

<MetricRow label="Before (measured in the audit week)">
	<Metric value="6.2" unit="days" label="Job done to invoice sent" context="Averaged over the previous six months." />
	<Metric value="34%" label="Missed calls, work hours" context="Went to voicemail. Techs checked when they could." />
	<Metric value="3.1" unit="days" label="Site visit to quote sent" context="Some ran a full week." />
	<Metric value="22%" label="Follow-up rate on stale quotes" context="Anything past 48 hours mostly fell off." />
	<Metric value="~380" unit="hrs / yr" label="Marcus’s admin time" context="Almost all of it after 8pm." />
</MetricRow>

Nothing on that list is unusual for a shop this size that has run on paper for a long time. The hours after dinner are the ones that stand out to the owner. Marcus had stopped answering the phone at 8pm because there was nothing he could do with the calls that came in.

## What the engagement did

The order of operations is the argument. Nothing was installed in phase three that phase one hadn't made possible.

