## What changed, measured the same way

<MetricRow label="After (measured in the fourteenth week)">
	<Metric value="0.4" unit="days" label="Job done to invoice sent" context="Down from 6.2. Same-day for most jobs." />
	<Metric value="4%" label="Missed calls, work hours" context="After-hours goes through triage; work-hours dispatch caught the rest." />
	<Metric value="0.6" unit="days" label="Site visit to quote sent" context="Techs draft in the truck, review, send. Same day for most." />
	<Metric value="100%" label="Follow-up rate on stale quotes" context="Automated sequence runs 48h, day 5, day 12." />
	<Metric value="~140" unit="hrs / yr" label="Marcus’s admin time" context="Down from 380. Most of it now Sunday review of the Monday brief." />
</MetricRow>

<BeforeAfter
	label="One workflow, one honest comparison"
	before="Dispatcher rewrites the schedule every morning on a whiteboard. Techs photograph it for the day."
	after="One live schedule. Every tech sees changes in the field. Dispatcher spends the recovered hour on parts orders."
	beforeSub="~5 hrs / week of manual rewriting."
	afterSub="~0 hrs / week."
/>

## What we did not install

The pieces that stayed out matter as much as the pieces that went in. The team looked at a website chatbot for two afternoons and could not find a single sample transcript from vendor demos that they would have wanted a Halden customer to receive. Same story for AI-generated marketing content: Marcus read three drafts, said they read like every other HVAC page in Missouri, and asked us to skip it. Those were the right calls. The point of the engagement was not "install more AI." It was to make the shop run cleanly enough that the AI we did install had a chance of being useful.

<Quote
	body="I stopped answering the phone at 8pm because there was nothing I could do about the calls that came in. Now the system handles them and I sleep."
	attribution="Marcus Halden, owner"
	illustrative
/>

## The point the shop wanted made

Marcus put it plainly in the final review: what changed was not that the shop got smarter. What changed was that the pieces the shop already knew how to do stopped falling on the floor between them. AI helped in two specific places. The rest of the recovered hours came from the paper going away.
